  "articleBody": "Renovating a foreclosed property bathroom can be rewarding, but it's easy to stumble into common pitfalls. One big mistake is *underestimating hidden damage*. What looks like a cosmetic fix on the surface can hide serious plumbing issues or even mold. Always start with a full, detailed inspection. Another common error is *skipping permits* when fixture moves or electrical/plumbing changes are involved. This can lead to big problems down the road with inspections or even resale. Don't forget to secure those! Beginning work without clear title or possession is also a huge risk that can halt your project. Ensure all the legal paperwork is sorted before swinging a hammer. Finally, failing to get a detailed scope of work and an itemized bid from your contractor can lead to budget overruns and misunderstandings. Always prioritize contractors who provide a clear process for surprises and proof of liability insurance. For anything involving plumbing, electrical, or structural changes, a licensed professional is really important.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}